## Why This Is Asked
Tests budgeted DP on strings and reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][j][d] encodes a subproblem under a deletion budget; 
- palindrome subsequence logic with matches/skips; 
- reconstruction paths under deletion budget.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalWithDeletions(s, k){
  const n=s.length; 
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][i][0]=1; for(let d=1; d<=k; d++) dp[i][i][d]=1; }
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      for(let d=0; d<=k; d++){
        if(s[i]===s[j]){
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j-1][d]+2, (d>0?dp[i+1][j][d-1]:0), (d>0?dp[i][j-1][d-1]:0));
        } else {
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j][d], dp[i][j-1][d], (d>0?dp[i+1][j-1][d-1]:0));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...Array.from({length:k+1},(_,d)=>dp[0][n-1][d]));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can space be reduced to O(n^2)?
- How to reconstruct the deletion indices?
- Change objective to count distinct palindromic subsequences with budget.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a DP with two choices per step (1- or 2-length blocks), plus reconstruction and complexity awareness.

## Key Concepts

- DP with two dimensions (i, t)
- Transitions for 1-length and 2-length blocks
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimization

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      // 1-length block ending at i-1
      if(dp[i-1][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 1; }
      }
      // 2-length block ending at i-1
      if(i>=2 && dp[i-2][t-1] !== NEG){
        const val = dp[i-2][t-1] + A[i-2] + A[i-1];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; take[i][t] = 2; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back from (n, k)
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reconstruct the exact block endpoints efficiently?
- How would you optimize space to O(k)?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for array partitioning with a per-segment objective function. It combines partition DP with range maximum queries and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over array partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation table M[a][b])
- Path reconstruction using parent pointers
- Time/space complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartition(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length-1; // 1-indexed array
  const M = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));

  // Precompute range maximums
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    M[i][i] = A[i];
    for(let j=i+1; j<=n; j++){
      M[i][j] = Math.max(M[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
    for(let i=t; i<=n; i++){
      for(let j=t-1; j<i; j++){
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + M[j+1][i] - c;
        if(val > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct segments
  const segments = [];
  let curr = n;
  for(let t=k; t>=1; t--){
    const prev = par[curr][t];
    segments.unshift([prev+1, curr]);
    curr = prev;
  }

  return {value: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Optimization Opportunities
- Use sparse table for O(1) range max queries with O(n log n) preprocessing
- Apply divide and conquer optimization if applicable
- Consider rolling array optimization to reduce space to O(n)

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests deriving a solvable DP for contiguous partitions with a length-based cost, plus a linear-time optimization and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partition DP
- Prefix sums and O(nk) optimization
- Backtracking to report segment endpoints

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTotalWithCost(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    let bestVal = -Infinity, bestIdx = -1;
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      const j = i - 1;
      if (dp[j][t-1] > -Infinity) {
        const cand = dp[j][t-1] - S[j] + c * j;
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestIdx = j; }
      }
      if (bestVal > -Infinity) {
        dp[i][t] = S[i] - c * i + c + bestVal;
        par[i][t] = bestIdx;
      }
    }
  }
  const segments = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  if (dp[n][k] <= -Infinity/2) return {best: -Infinity, segments: []};
  while (t > 0) {
    const j = par[i][t];
    segments.push([j+1, i]);
    i = j; t--;
  }
  segments.reverse();
  return {best: dp[n][k], segments};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if c is paid only for non-first element or per segment boundary?
- How would you extend to maximize total value for at most k segments instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a 3D DP on a grid with an exact count constraint and path reconstruction, a realistic routing/optimization pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) with 3 dimensions
- Exact-count handling via -inf initialization
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers
- Space optimizations via rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, boost, K){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const neg = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(neg)));
  const par = Array.from({length:n}, () => Array.from({length:m}, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  const b00 = boost[0][0] ? 1 : 0;
  dp[0][0][b00] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for (let j=0;j<m;j++){
      for (let t=0;t<=K;t++){
        const add = boost[i][j] ? 1 : 0;
        if (i===0 && j===0) continue;
        const candFromTop = i>0 ? dp[i-1][j][t - add] : neg;
        const candFromLeft = j>0 ? dp[i][j-1][t - add] : neg;
        const bestPrev = Math.max(candFromTop, candFromLeft);
        if (bestPrev > neg/2) {
          dp[i][j][t] = bestPrev + grid[i][j];
          par[i][j][t] = candFromTop >= candFromLeft ? ['top'] : ['left'];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n-1][m-1][K], path: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the actual path coordinates efficiently?
- How would you optimize memory to O(m*K) and still reconstruct the path?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an optimized DP on a circular constraint and track the number of optimal solutions.

## Key Concepts
- DP on linear sequences with spacing (dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-d] + v[i]))
- Reducing circularity to two linear cases (include first vs exclude first)
- Counting distinct optimal subsets with tie handling (sum counts when equal)

## Code Example
```javascript
function linearDP(arr, d){
  const n = arr.length; const dp = Array(n+1).fill(0); const ways = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    const take = arr[i-1] + (i-d>=0 ? dp[i-d] : 0);
    const skip = dp[i-1];
    if(take>skip){ dp[i]=take; ways[i]=(i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1); }
    else if(take<skip){ dp[i]=skip; ways[i]=ways[i-1]; }
    else { dp[i]=skip; ways[i] = ((i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1) + ways[i-1]); }
  }
  return [dp[n], ways[n]];
}

function maxCircular(v, d){
  const [bestA, cntA] = linearDP(v.slice(1), d); // exclude first
  const n = v.length;
  const seg = (n > 2*d-1) ? v.slice(d, n-d+1) : [];
  const [bestBseg, cntBseg] = seg.length ? linearDP(seg, d) : [0,1];
  const bestB = v[0] + bestBseg;
  if(bestA > bestB) return [bestA, cntA];
  if(bestB > bestA) return [bestB, cntBseg];
  return [bestA, cntA + cntBseg];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow a variable distance per pair or weighted distance penalties?
- How would you test correctness and performance on worst-case inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP with a constrained path problem and reconstruction, a common real interview topic for large-scale environments like PayPal or Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP state: i, j, t, dir (0 right, 1 down)
- Turn counting via transition from one dir to the other
- Base cases for first row/column
- Path reconstruction pointers and memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) dp[0][j][0][0] = dp[0][j-1][0][0] + grid[0][j];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) dp[i][0][0][1] = dp[i-1][0][0][1] + grid[i][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromLeftSame = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromLeftTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromLeftSame, fromLeftTurn));
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromTopSame = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromTopTurn = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : NEG;
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], grid[i][j] + Math.max(fromTopSame, fromTopTurn));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = NEG, bestT = 0, bestDir = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
      if (dp[n-1][m-1][t][d] > best) {
        best = dp[n-1][m-1][t][d]; bestT = t; bestDir = d;
      }
    }
  }
  return {best, bestT, bestDir};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the path coordinates efficiently?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n*m*k) or O(k) with rolling arrays?

